===== HEADER (added by Lane B, not by the script) =====
Run: node apps/client/e2e/browser-tutor.mjs (KEEP_PROFILE=1, second attempt of two)
Fixture: es-fabulas-samaniego, es-419, Chromium --enable-unsafe-webgpu --use-angle=metal,
on an M-series Mac. navigator.gpu adapter: present (see t+4.4s line below).

What ran on WebGPU vs wasm:
  - STT (whisper-base, encoder fp16 + decoder q8): WebGPU  (stt_load_ms=2023ms; see t+76.3s)
  - LLM (Qwen3-1.7B-q4f16_1-MLC via @mlc-ai/web-llm): WebGPU only, no wasm path exists for
    WebLLM (llm_load_ms=2707ms; see t+79.0s)
  - TTS (Kokoro 82M): not exercised in this run — the fixture's learning locale is es-419,
    which has no synthesized voice in this build (verified, not unattempted — see
    planning/BROWSER-TUTOR.md's Slice 2+3 status note and worker.ts's HONEST LABEL comment
    above loadTts). English TTS was separately proven end-to-end with a standalone Node
    script (kokoro-js generate(), af_heart voice): 2.975s of audio, RMS 0.067, non-silent,
    saved to a wav file — see the Lane B report for that script's output.

Per-stage latencies observed (this run):
  - Model download (STT 95MB + LLM 1.1GB + TTS 90MB = 1285MB total): 69.3s
  - STT pipeline load: 2023ms
  - LLM engine load: 2707ms
  - STT transcription of a 2.7s clip: 21152ms  <-- see ESCALATION below
  - LLM turn (first token through full reply, tool-call fallback triggered): well under 5s
    once STT handed it text at all

ESCALATION (per this lane's own stop-and-report rule: "per-turn latency > 20s on the path
that works"): STT transcription latency regressed from 1.05s/2.7s-clip in the slice-1
evidence log to ~21s/2.7s-clip here, TWICE, reproducibly (21768ms on the first full run,
21152ms on this one), and the transcribed text became garbage repeated-token output
("de de de de de de...") instead of the correct Spanish sentence. STT alone worked
correctly and fast in slice 1 with no LLM loaded. The only pipeline change between "STT
alone, fast, correct" and "STT + LLM both resident, slow, garbage" is that a 1.1GB WebGPU
LLM model is now also resident in the same GPU context during transcription — the leading
hypothesis (not yet proven with a controlled STT-only-after-LLM-load repro, which this
report flags as the next diagnostic step) is WebGPU resource contention/memory pressure
between the two models degrading whisper's numerics. This did not block the pipeline
end-to-end — the LLM still received *some* text and replied coherently in Spanish — but it
is a real, reproducible quality/latency regression that should be root-caused before this
ships as anything but experimental. Everything else observed in this run (download,
model loads, tool-calling-unsupported fallback, <think> suppression via
extra_body.enable_thinking, sentence-chunked captions, state transitions) worked as
designed.

Also of note: the `[browser-tutor] FAILED: page.waitForTimeout: ...` at the end of this log
is this session's own deliberate `kill` of the script once the evidence above was captured,
not a pipeline failure — the fake-mic file's second utterance ("Guarda la palabra
cigarra.") and its tool round trip were not reached in this run because STT was still
producing garbage for the first utterance. A first attempt (before the WebLLM tool-calling
enable_thinking fix and an esbuild bundling fix — see the Lane B report) failed earlier,
during model download, with "worker_error: unknown" — root-caused to kokoro-js's
`dist/kokoro.js` statically importing Node's `path`/`fs/promises`, which esbuild does not
substitute via kokoro-js's own package.json `"browser"` field once a package also declares
an `"exports"` map, so both imports survived into the bundle as literal unresolvable ESM
specifiers and the `type: "module"` Worker failed to load at all. Fixed via an esbuild
`alias` in apps/client/scripts/build-tutor-worker.mjs pointing both specifiers at a tiny
local empty-module shim (apps/client/scripts/tutor-worker-node-shim.mjs).
